WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:10.099
We are, however, seeing some Republicans very
slowly, clearly for reasons of self-preservation,

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:16.049
starting to jump ship a little bit with the
wild fraud allegations that Donald Trump is

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.049
making.

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:18.340
I have two examples for you today.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.779
These are not good people.

00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:23.030
These are still people who deserve to never
vote again, but at work again.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:24.030
No, they can.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.030
I apologize.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.030
They obviously deserve to vote again.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:27.030
That's not what I meant.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.970
They deserve to never work again.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:34.640
We're going to start with former New Jersey
Governor Chris Christie backing off of Trump's

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.219
voter fraud claims, which are, of course,
bogus.

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:42.090
Christie, a top ally of Donald Trump, helped.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:46.379
Donald helped was a loose term, but worked
with Trump on debate prep.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:51.340
And yesterday, Chris Christie said, you know,
you can keep saying voter fraud.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.180
And if there is voter fraud, then we need
to deal with it.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.739
But you've got to produce some evidence here.

00:00:56.739 --> 00:01:00.199
And Chris Christie starting to distance Chris
Christie.

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:01.430
It should not have to be said.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.820
Counting absentee ballots is not illegal.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:03.820
It is not fraud.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:04.930
It is not corruption.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:10.200
George, let's say I've been fortunate enough
to be on the ballot a number of times myself.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.200
I've won.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.200
I've lost.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.480
And so as a candidate, you even more invested
in the integrity of the system.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.480
Right.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.580
So if this stuff is going on that the president
is talking about, all of us want to fight

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.369
it out because it would undercut everything
that we believe in our system.

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:31.759
But as a prosecutor, that's like asking me
to indict someone without showing me any evidence.

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:35.030
If you're going to say those things from behind
the podium at the White House, it's his right

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.030
to do it.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.280
It's his right to pursue legal action.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.579
But show us the evidence.

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:42.560
We heard nothing today about any evidence.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.530
I want to know what backs up what he said
so that I can analyze it.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.530
And let me tell you, if he's right, I'll be
outraged.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:49.770
And I'm sure you would be, too.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.469
If he's wrong and if he's wrong, then the
American people should be able to make the

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:59.729
judgment about this election that the results
have been fair and this kind of thing.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:03.729
All it does is inflame without informing.

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:07.380
And we cannot permit information without information.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:15.060
So Chris Christie was maybe the first prominent
actual Trump adviser to publicly confront

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:21.200
Trump and say what you're saying is not, at
least as of now, backed up by evidence.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.940
It's inflammatory.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.220
He said he already won when he didn't.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.950
He's saying that there's fraud when there
is no evidence of it.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.610
You've got to be careful with that.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:34.790
We then also heard from former Pennsylvania
Republican Senator Rick Santorum.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:41.910
Now, he is not a Trump adviser and has even
been a little lukewarm on Trump over the years.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.480
He is a Republican that you could reasonably
describe as pro Trump, although he has had

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.370
some hesitations.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:55.030
He said it was just completely wrong for Trump
to undermine public confidence in the election,

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.170
declare victory, all of that stuff.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:01.540
Rick Santorum, no Republican elected official,
is going to stand behind that statement.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:04.620
I mean, none of them will.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.650
They will stand behind elements of that statement.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:13.090
They will stand behind his comment about the
polling and how he can make the argument that

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:18.250
it was suppression and dishonest.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:26.670
I mean, you can stand behind the lack of transparency
when it came to allowing the votes to be counted.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:27.670
There's evidence of that.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:28.900
And he provided some evidence for that.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:34.700
There are certain things they will stand behind
in that statement because they reflect the

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:35.700
reality.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:47.200
But much of that statement was was not was
not factual and was at times incendiary and

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.660
and not something that the United States should
say or any elected officials.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:56.810
You said you have evidence to prove it.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:03.130
And at this point in time, we don't and we
won't until there is an investigation as to

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:04.130
what to happen.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:08.730
And for I got to tell you, I sat there and
I listened to him talking about the votes

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:13.530
being taken away from him, and then he shifted
to Arizona and said, hey, I can win.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.490
I win this thing if they count the votes.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:19.910
Well, how can you how can you say we have
to wait and count the votes in Arizona and

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:21.630
I can win this for making it.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:26.300
But if you count the votes in Philadelphia,
you're stealing them.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:33.250
The reality is in Pennsylvania, Democrats
voted by mail and Republicans voted by in

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:34.250
person.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:39.690
And because you asked them to do so shortly
after Trump wrapped up that bizarre press

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:40.690
conference.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.810
Santorum also said that the president's rhetoric
was.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:51.510
Dangerous and I think what's important to
remember here is that these are not good people

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.980
because they're doing this.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:05:00.010
Chris Christie supported Trump throughout
the entire campaign and even helped to manufacture

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:01.710
this exact situation.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.840
This is a really important point.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.500
Chris Christie now says, oh, you know, Trump's
got to be careful.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.300
He's got to bring the evidence.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:14.710
Christie facilitated the current crisis situation,
which Donald Trump has has fomented.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:20.280
These people are merely trying to self preserve,
but they all need to go.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.750
And at some point we'll put together a full
list of these people.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:28.800
Chris Christie, Kelly McCann, any of course,
you know, Don Jr., Ivanka Kushner, Steve Bannon,

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.330
the whole lot of them deserve to never work
again.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:40.920
These are facilitators for the most dishonest
and weaponized, least incompetent presidency

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.930
that we have had in the modern era.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:51.360
I could probably say of all time, but let's
just say in the modern era, and I hope everybody

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.481
has been taking names, let me put it that
way.

00:05:53.481 --> 00:05:54.481
So to take a little bit of a break from the
presidential race, we actually got some very

00:05:54.481 --> 00:05:55.481
good news on Tuesday.

00:05:55.481 --> 00:05:56.481
The war on drugs was crushed on Election Day,
at least as much as you could crush it in

00:05:56.481 --> 00:05:57.481
one day.

00:05:57.481 --> 00:05:58.481
In every state where marijuana legalization
or decriminalization or progressive ization

00:05:58.481 --> 00:05:59.481
of drug policy was on the ballot, it won in
Arizona, Montana, New Jersey and South Dakota,

00:05:59.481 --> 00:06:00.481
voters legalized recreational marijuana.

00:06:00.481 --> 00:06:01.481
Incredible in Mississippi and even South Dakota
voters legalized medical marijuana.

00:06:01.481 --> 00:06:02.481
South Dakota had both legalized recreational
and legalized medical.

00:06:02.481 --> 00:06:03.481
In Oregon, voters decriminalized but not legalized
all drugs, including cocaine and heroin.

00:06:03.481 --> 00:06:04.481
Also in Oregon, voters legalized the use of
psilocybin, a psychedelic drug found in so-called

00:06:04.481 --> 00:06:05.481
magic mushrooms in specifically supervised
therapeutic uses.

00:06:05.481 --> 00:06:06.481
This is huge.

00:06:06.481 --> 00:06:07.481
Oregon also is.

00:06:07.481 --> 00:06:08.481
So what's going to happen in Oregon is you
will no longer be punished with jail or prison

00:06:08.481 --> 00:06:09.481
time for small amounts of possession of any
drug that includes cocaine and heroin.

00:06:09.481 --> 00:06:10.481
If you get caught with cocaine and heroin,
you can either pay a hundred dollars fine

00:06:10.481 --> 00:06:11.481
or do a completed health assessment, the details
of which I don't yet know.

00:06:11.481 --> 00:06:12.481
But we will find out.

00:06:12.481 --> 00:06:13.481
In Washington, D.C., voters decriminalized
psychedelic plants also following following

00:06:13.481 --> 00:06:14.481
the lead of several other cities.

00:06:14.481 --> 00:06:15.481
And again, Oregon, the first state in the
United States in modern times to decriminalize

00:06:15.481 --> 00:06:16.481
all drugs.

00:06:16.481 --> 00:06:17.481
Marijuana is now legalized in 15 states, plus
Washington, D.C., although Washington, D.C.

00:06:17.481 --> 00:06:18.481
still does not allow sales, all measures won
by at least five percentage points in every

00:06:18.481 --> 00:06:19.481
state.

00:06:19.481 --> 00:06:20.481
This really is not hugely shocking.

00:06:20.481 --> 00:06:21.481
Over the last decade, we've seen polls show
marijuana legalization is increasingly popular

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:22.481
in many polls.

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:23.481
Even among Republicans, there is support for
at least marijuana decriminalization.

00:06:23.481 --> 00:06:24.481
This is a big rejection of the war on drugs.

00:06:24.481 --> 00:06:25.481
It starts to signal a major shift in U.S.
drug policy.

00:06:25.481 --> 00:06:26.481
It's going to push for more of the disassembly
of the war on drugs.

00:06:26.481 --> 00:06:27.481
But another thing that's important to remember
is we are getting the first steps here, but

00:06:27.481 --> 00:06:28.481
we still need to deal with the criminal justice
component.

00:06:28.481 --> 00:06:29.481
So, for example, if anybody has a record now
for in Oregon, if you have a record for possession

00:06:29.481 --> 00:06:30.481
of small amounts of cocaine, which now are
legal, decriminalized, I should say, not legalize

00:06:30.481 --> 00:06:31.481
decriminalized, what happens to your record?

00:06:31.481 --> 00:06:32.481
It should probably be expunged.

00:06:32.481 --> 00:06:33.481
And that is the next step.

00:06:33.481 --> 00:06:34.481
That is the next step here.

00:06:34.481 --> 00:06:35.481
That's the next forefront.

00:06:35.481 --> 00:06:36.481
Legal cannabis.

00:06:36.481 --> 00:06:37.481
You know, nice for people looking to go to
a nice store and get, you know, curated, curated

00:06:37.481 --> 00:06:38.481
pot.

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:39.481
But the criminal justice piece is more difficult
and it has to come right after this.

00:06:39.481 --> 00:06:40.481
And hopefully we will see that.

00:06:40.481 --> 00:06:41.481
Also, I expect to see the tobacco industry
get more and more into cannabis.

00:06:41.481 --> 00:06:42.481
And there's also real opportunities in terms
of taking the tax revenue that this will generate

00:06:42.481 --> 00:06:43.481
and really doing great things with it.

00:06:43.481 --> 00:06:44.481
We're going to be discussing it more.

00:06:44.481 --> 00:06:45.481
We're going to be looking to places like Colorado,
even Massachusetts, as potential models of

00:06:45.481 --> 00:06:47.830
how this can be managed, how the money can
be used.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:49.960
But a major win against the war on drugs on
Tuesday.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.430
And we'll keep talking about ballot initiatives
because there's a lot of really interesting

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:51.490
stuff to discuss.

